## Summary

`pnpm update <pkg>` fails when the lockfile pins a version the registry no longer serves, such as an unpublished release. The lockfile verification gate checks every locked entry against `minimumReleaseAge` (and the other supply-chain policies) before resolution, and the missing version fails as `could not be checked against minimumReleaseAge (version not present in registry manifest)`. The update that would replace the entry never runs.

This PR has the gate skip the policy verifiers (`minimumReleaseAge`, `trustPolicy`) for the lockfile entries the update replaces. The resolver re-picks those entries and applies the same policies to what it picks. The skip applies only when all of these hold:

- the command is an update that selects packages by name (`pnpm update <pkg>`, the resulting `updateMatching` / `DropOnly` targets);
- there is no `--depth` limit;
- the update covers every importer in the lockfile, so no importer keeps the old pin;
- the entry's name and version match the targets of every importer.

Everything else is still verified as before: `pnpm install` and `pnpm add`, `pnpm update` with `--depth`, filtered updates (`--filter`) that leave another importer's pin in place, and every locked entry the update does not target. The offline resolution-shape and dependency-alias checks still run on skipped entries. A verification run that skipped any entry is not recorded in the verification cache, so the next install that reuses the lockfile verifies every en
